From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Conway <dylan.conway567@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 18:43:34 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Fix startup SIGILL on Linux arm64 when SVE2 is reported
 without SVE (#45099)

### What does this PR do?

Bun on Linux arm64 dies with an illegal instruction at startup on
machines where the kernel reports the SVE2 CPU flag but not the base SVE
flag. Bun now uses SVE2 only when both flags are set.

Affected kernels set `HWCAP2_SVE2` from one CPU register field without
checking that SVE exists. The kernel fixed this in
[064737920bdb](https://github.com/torvalds/linux/commit/064737920bdb). A
Linux VM on an Apple M4 (SME, no SVE) is one affected setup.

| Kernel versions with the bug |
|---|
| 5.4 - 6.6.78 |
| 6.7 - 6.12.13 |
| 6.13 - 6.13.2 |

Highway, the SIMD library behind Bun's string search, read `HWCAP2_SVE2`
alone and then ran `cntb`, an SVE instruction, to measure the vector
length. The fix is one condition in `hwy/targets.cc`, carried as
`patches/highway/sve2-requires-sve.patch` because upstream master has
the same code. On a kernel whose flags agree, the result is unchanged.
simdutf, simdjson, vectorscan and Rust's standard library make the same
check.

### How did you verify your code works?

- Bun 1.4.3 arm64 under `qemu-aarch64` 7.2 with `-cpu max,sve=off`,
which reports these flags: SIGILL at the `cntb`.
- Highway's detection built alone with the pinned clang, before and
after the patch, on six qemu CPU models: only `max,sve=off` changes
(SIGILL, then `NEON_BF16`). `max,sve-max-vq=1` still gets `SVE2_128`.
- The kernel table comes from reading `arch/arm64/kernel/cpufeature.c`
at 5.4.302, 5.10.240, 5.15.189, 6.1.189, the last release of 6.2 to 6.5
and 6.7 to 6.11, 6.14, and the releases on each side of the fix in 6.6,
6.12 and 6.13. Nothing older than 5.4 was read, nor the other 5.x series
or 6.0.

No test is added. Not verified: an arm64 build of this branch has not
been run on an affected machine.
