Subject: [PATCH] TextEncoder: fix `encode` of a substring whose parent string
 changed width (#45281)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

### What does this PR do?

`TextEncoder.prototype.encode` returned the wrong bytes for the result
of `slice`/`substring` when the string it was cut from had switched
between 8-bit and 16-bit storage after the cut.

A substring points into its parent string and remembers the parent's
width from the time it was created. Using the parent as a property key
replaces its storage with the equal string already in the atom table,
and that string can have the other width. `encode` chose between the
8-bit and 16-bit encoder from the substring's remembered width, then
read the characters through `view()`, which has the parent's current
width.

- 8-bit to 16-bit: the 16-bit characters were read as bytes.
- 16-bit to 8-bit: the 8-bit characters were read as 16-bit units, which
reads past the end of the string.

```js
const table = { ["abĀcdefgh".replace(/Ā/g, "")]: 1 }; // key with 16-bit storage
const base = ["ab", "cdefgh"].join(""); // same characters, 8-bit storage
const part = base.slice(2, 7);
const enc = s => new TextEncoder().encode(s).join();
console.log(enc(part), table[base], enc(part));
// 1.4.3:   99,100,101,102,103 1 99,0,100,0,101
// this PR: 99,100,101,102,103 1 99,100,101,102,103
```

`encode` now picks the encoder from the view it is about to read, as
`encodeInto` already does. The fast path for ropes that are not
substrings is unchanged.

### How did you verify your code works?

Two new tests in `test/js/web/encoding/text-encoder.test.js`, one per
direction. Both fail with `USE_SYSTEM_BUN=1 bun test` (1.4.3) and the
whole file passes with `bun bd test`. The script above prints the same
bytes as Node on the debug build.
