From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Conway <dylan.conway567@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 16:45:06 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] sockets: a peer that keeps the receive buffer full no longer
 stops timers (#45278)

A socket whose peer sends faster than its `data` handler runs could hold
the event loop forever: timers, immediates and everything else stopped
until the peer slowed down. On POSIX, every kind of socket on the loop
is affected (`Bun.connect`, `node:net`, `node:http`).

After a nearly full 512 KB `recv`, the read loop in
`us_internal_dispatch_ready_poll` reads again without going back to the
event loop. The limit of 10 repeats only applied when more than 2 polls
were ready. With one busy socket there was no limit, and a peer that
refills the kernel buffer while the handler runs keeps every read full.

The limit now always applies, so one readable event is at most 11 reads.

```js
// server: endless "HTTP/1.1 100 Continue\r\n\r\n", as fast as the socket drains
const req = http.get({ host: "127.0.0.1", port });
setTimeout(() => req.destroy(), 2000);
```

`strace` of that client on 1.4.3: 8 `epoll_pwait2` calls, then 476 full
`recvfrom` calls in a row until it was killed.

Linux x64, release build without LTO. "No limit" is the same binary with
the limit lifted.

| | this PR | no limit | node 26.7 |
|---|---|---|---|
| snippet above, 2000 ms timer, 8 runs | 2005 to 2067 ms | never fired
in 3 of 8 | 2003 to 2006 ms |
| `node:net`, 1 ms `data` handler, 500 ms timer, 6 runs | 503 to 512 ms
| never fired in 6 of 6 | 502 to 506 ms |
| new test, 5 runs | passes 5 | fails 5 (14 reads in one turn) | |

The new test in `socket.test.ts` sets an 8 MB `SO_RCVBUF` and a 1 MB
`SO_SNDBUF` with `setSocketOptions`, keeps the client paused until the
kernel holds 14 full reads, then counts `data()` calls between two
`setImmediate` callbacks. It expects 14 reads in total and 11 as the
most in one turn. The same shape as a script, 30 runs each: 14 in one
turn on 1.4.3 and 11 with the fix, on both Linux x64 and macOS arm64.

Linux caps both options at `net.core.rmem_max` and `wmem_max` without
reporting it, and at the default 212992 the payload cannot be queued.
The test is skipped on such a host, which includes the Debian 13 and
Ubuntu 25.04 CI lanes. It runs on the Alpine and macOS lanes.

Bulk receive, `Bun.connect` with a crc32 of each chunk, 8 GB per run, 12
runs each in rotated order, medians: 13.94 and 14.00 GB/s with the limit
(measured twice as a control), 13.97 GB/s without. CPU per GB: 70.7 and
70.8 ms with, 71.1 ms without.
