Subject: [PATCH] fetch over HTTP/2: reject on a TLS alert after the handshake,
 and stop using a connection that went silent after an abort (#45422)
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Two fixes for `fetch()` over HTTP/2
(`BUN_FEATURE_FLAG_EXPERIMENTAL_HTTP2_CLIENT`). Both are cases where a
request hangs, and later requests to the same origin hang with it.

handshake

A TLS 1.3 client finishes its handshake before the server checks the
client certificate. When the server then sends a fatal alert (for
example `certificate_required`), uSockets reports it as a failed
handshake on a socket that the HTTP/2 session already owns.
`on_handshake` only handled sockets owned by an `HTTPClient`, so it
marked the socket dead and closed it without telling the session.

The request never settled. Its abort-tracker entry was dropped, so
`abort()` had no effect. The session stayed registered, so later
requests to the origin joined it and hung as well.

The session now fails with the same error as HTTP/1.1, `EPROTO`.

The other handshake event such a socket can get, a TLS 1.2 renegotiation
that finished, took the same path to the same hang. It now fails the
session with `HTTP2ProtocolError`, since RFC 9113 §9.2.1 makes a
renegotiation a connection error of type `PROTOCOL_ERROR`.

Bun 1.4.3 does not hang here (it rejects with `ECONNRESET`), so the hang
has not been in a release.

When a connection dies with no FIN or RST (a network change, a dropped
NAT entry), the kernel keeps accepting writes for many minutes. An abort
only sent `RST_STREAM` and kept the session, so each later request to
the origin joined it, or took it from the keep-alive pool, and hung
until its own timeout. The client never sent a PING, and with `timeout:
false` on every stream no timer ran at all. HTTP/1.1 does not have this
problem, because an abort closes the socket.

- When the session has read nothing for a second, and the aborted
request's stream (if it has one yet) is at least a second old, a PING
goes out with the `RST_STREAM`.
- Until the ACK arrives the session takes no new stream and is not
pooled, so new requests use another connection. The ACK puts the session
back in use.
- While the PING is unanswered the session's idle timer is capped at 15
seconds (16 to 20 on the 4-second timer wheel), which is Go's default
`PingTimeout`, or at `BUN_CONFIG_HTTP_IDLE_TIMEOUT` if that is shorter.
What re-arms the idle timer re-arms the cap: progress on a stream, a
writable event, a request body chunk, another abort. So does DATA for a
stream that was reset, which can sit in front of the ACK.
- When that timer fires, the connection is closed and the streams still
on it fail with `ECONNRESET`. A request whose own shorter `timeout` runs
out first still fails with a `TimeoutError`.
- gRPC servers close a connection that sends several PINGs with no
HEADERS or DATA from the server in between (golang/go#70575), which
would take the other streams down. So only one liveness PING goes out
between responses. The server counts per connection, so the keep-alive
pool does not give the PING back; HEADERS or DATA do, also those for a
stream that was already reset. A second such abort retires the session
as a GOAWAY would: no new stream, and closed when its last stream ends.
- `FetchSession.close()` also closes a connection that has no request
left and only waits for the ACK.

Any other abort changes nothing.

12 new tests in `test/js/web/fetch/fetch-http2-client.test.ts`, run
locally on Linux x64 only.

| | `main` (CI's release build of 116dd64fd) | this branch
(`release-asan` build) |
| --- | --- | --- |
| TLS 1.3 fatal alert after the handshake | hangs | rejects with
`EPROTO` |
| TLS 1.2 renegotiation on an h2 connection (Node is the peer) | hangs |
rejects with `HTTP2ProtocolError` |
| all 12 new tests | | 12 pass |

The first push (69ee9537b) was green on every lane that finished in
build 126227, apart from `bun-install-windows-rename-retry.test.ts` on
Windows aarch64. That test also fails on `main` (build 126172) and on 8
more of the 30 builds before this one.

The third commit fixes what the review found. Each fix has a test that I
ran before and after it:

| | 69ee9537b | c79a222c2 |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `FetchSession.close()` with a PING unanswered | the connection stays
open | closed |
| a request's own `timeout` runs out with a PING unanswered |
`ECONNRESET` (`main`: `TimeoutError`) | `TimeoutError` |
| silent aborts in a row, with the pool in between | a PING each time |
one PING, then a new connection |
| the test of the unanswered PING | 20 s | 9 s |

Also green on this branch's ASAN build, with no ASAN report: the rest of
`fetch-http2-client.test.ts` (88 tests in all), `fetch-session`,
`fetch-http2-adversarial`, `fetch-http2-leak`, `fetch.tls`,
`fetch-keepalive`, `fetch-abort-socket-close-race`,
`fetch-abort-queued`.

`fetch-tls-abortsignal-timeout` has a 50 ms wall-clock limit and fails
on my loaded machine with and without the third commit (11 failures in 6
runs without, 4 with). It does not turn the HTTP/2 client on.

I used a `release-asan` build and not `bun bd`. The liveness tests sleep
past the one-second grace period because nothing observable marks that
moment. They start the sleep when the test server has seen the request.
