From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenSec <contactus@opensec.in>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 02:57:13 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] Pi-Bolt: ahead-of-time compiled executables

Bun's changes for Pi-Bolt's executables, on Linux x86-64, macOS ARM64 and Windows x64:

- Executables with a prebuilt heap and a code image, made by `bun build --compile` and mapped from the
  executable when it starts (bundler, StandaloneModuleGraph, ZigSourceProvider).
- Builtin modules imported in the order Node evaluates them; the names of a builtin module required in
  a wrapper read once, ahead of the wrapper's dependencies.
- Stack traces and crash reports of ahead-of-time code: no CodeBlocks made to read a frame, crash
  reports not uploaded unasked. The default Error.prepareStackTrace takes any object.
- Memory: garbage collection markers for the prebuilt heap, the allocator's idle sweep every 250 ms,
  static strings not reference counted from Rust; on macOS a starting heap budget that fits the
  prebuilt heap, and a collection every 5 seconds while the program waits.
- fetch: an idle connection waits in the keep-alive pool for 4 seconds, or as long as the server says.
- macOS: the prebuilt heap and code image mapped from the __BUN section, with the executable where it
  was linked to be; SharedArrayBuffer with the JIT off; a terminal reopened by its own device, which
  kqueue can watch; the programs an executable starts are started by Pi-Bolt's launcher helper.

- Windows x64: the prebuilt heap and code image in the executable's PE sections, mapped read-only where
  nothing writes; DLLs that are delay-loaded only from System32 (a delay-load hook, the dependent load
  flag, WIC); a build without the JIT stays without it, and the debugging options that reach memory or
  load code are off in a compiled program; crash reports of compiled code; a collection every second
  while waiting (5 s measured +40% CPU while a reply streams), and mimalloc's purge delay left at its
  default; an idle keep-alive connection aged on GetTickCount64; the writer of the prebuilt heap takes a word that
  looks like an address of the blocks or the build's scratch memory for a number unless it is aligned and its page committed,
  and says so.

Includes the engine changes of Pi-Bolt 0.7.1 to 0.7.3 on pi-bolt (5f6ad773e, c9bde4b67): writev for
queued writes, mimalloc from upstream, less idle work, an idle keep-alive's age on a clock nobody sets,
fetch through a TLS 1.2 renegotiation.

Includes the engine changes of Pi-Bolt 0.7.4 (bolt-v0.7.4): code built into an executable resolves no module or package in the working directory (absolute paths and require.resolve paths still do); internal names and messages cleaned up. Builds on Linux and macOS: the executable's bytes mutable only where Windows rewrites them.

Includes the engine changes of Pi-Bolt 0.8.5: Latin-1 strings are converted to UTF-8 in one pass into
a buffer of the exact size (request bodies). From upstream Bun: sliceAnsi and stripANSI of huge input;
FileSink kept alive until its close callback returns, and its microtask checkpoint; PipeReader stops
after a failed read; stdio with no FileSink writes synchronously; a blob owns its string; children's
stdout and stderr non-blocking, with EAGAIN handled; spawnSync no longer points the VM at the loop it
waits on; the idle full collection also has mimalloc give back the memory it keeps.

Windows (0.8.5): the sockets' sweep timer stops with the last socket, so an idle process no longer wakes every 4 seconds
(oven-sh/bun#37096), and a task left queued after a tick wakes the poll (oven-sh/bun#42968). On every platform the
runtime transpiler cache's default folder is @t@pi-bolt, beside stock Bun's @t@, whose entries the two runtimes took for
stale and rewrote in turn.
