From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dylan Conway <dylan.conway567@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 21 Oct 2026 17:28:46 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] fetch, RedisClient: a TLS session that breaks after its
 handshake is a lost connection, not a failed handshake (#46409)

Restores two recoveries that 1.4.3 has and `main` lost: `fetch` sending
a request again after a reused https connection breaks, and
`Bun.RedisClient` reconnecting after a `rediss://` connection breaks.

When a TLS session that is already up gets bytes it cannot accept (bytes
that are not a TLS record, a record that does not authenticate) and the
peer closes:

| | 1.4.3 | `main` |
|---|---|---|
| `fetch`, GET on a reused https connection | sent again on a new
connection, resolves | rejects with `EPROTO: The TLS handshake failed` |
| `fetch`, GET through a reused CONNECT tunnel, the session with the
**https proxy** breaks | sent again, resolves | rejects with `EPROTO` |
| `RedisClient` over `rediss://`, a command in flight or the connection
idle | reconnects | never reconnects, every later command rejects with
`Connection has failed` |

A FIN, an RST and a close_notify at the same moment are recovered from
on both builds.

Since #44618, `ssl_fail_established_session`
(`packages/bun-usockets/src/crypto/openssl.c`) reports a fatal error on
an established session to the socket's owner through
`on_handshake(success = 0)`, and then closes the socket itself. An owner
that takes every such report for a refused handshake fails for good,
before the close event that carries its recovery policy arrives:

- `HTTPContext::Handler::on_handshake` called `close_and_fail`, so
`HTTPClient::on_close`, which holds the resend rule, found a client that
had already failed.
- `JSValkeyClient`'s `on_handshake` called `fail_with_js_value`, which
sets `flags.failed`, and `ValkeyClient::on_close` skips the retry policy
for a failed client.

The WebSocket client got its guard for this in #45720.

**fetch** (`src/http/HTTPContext.rs`, the fixing lines): a failed report
for a request that `on_close` would send again returns without failing
the client. uSockets closes the socket right after the report, and
`on_close` does what it does for a FIN. `allow_retry` is only ever set
on a reused connection, which is past its handshake, so a real handshake
failure never takes this path. To ask the same question in both places,
the condition moves out of `on_close` into
`HTTPClient::retries_on_close()` unchanged (`src/http/lib.rs`).

Everything that is not sent again keeps `main`'s behavior and its
`EPROTO`: a POST, a stream body, a response whose body has started,
`keepalive: false`.

**RedisClient** (`src/runtime/valkey_jsc/`): a failed report on a
connection that is `Connected` (its `HELLO` was answered), with
`autoReconnect` on, is a lost connection. The commands in flight are
rejected with the TLS reason, as on `main`, `failed` stays unset, and
the close that follows runs the usual retry policy. The rejections are
made without entering an event loop scope, so they are observed after
`on_close` has run: a command issued from a `catch` waits for the new
connection, as it does after a FIN, instead of going out on the dead
socket.

Two cases keep `main`'s behavior, the client fails and every pending
command is rejected with the TLS reason:

- **Before the reply to `HELLO`.** That is where a TLS 1.3 server
refuses the client's certificate, after the client's side of the
handshake is over, so a failure there is a refusal and not a lost
connection. A client with no certificate gets `EPROTO:
...TLSV1_ALERT_CERTIFICATE_REQUIRED` from its first dial. (1.4.3 keeps
dialing here: 9 connections in 8 s, with the command still pending.)
- **`autoReconnect: false`.** There is no policy to hand the close to,
and failing the client tells the commands that are still queued why, not
only the ones in flight.

- **The session inside a CONNECT tunnel** (origin side, `ProxyTunnel`).
A request on a reused tunnel whose inner session ends is not sent again
on `main`, but it is not on 1.4.3 either, and not for a FIN or a
close_notify either. It is a different cause (`ProxyTunnel::on_close`
never consults the resend rule) and older than #44618, so it is not part
of this PR.
- **HTTP/2 sessions**: `fail_streams` sends nothing again for any kind
of close, so only the error code depends on the report.
- **`Bun.SQL`**: the pool does not retry the close of an established
connection whatever the error is, and dials again for the next query.
- A finished TLS 1.2 renegotiation makes `RedisClient` send `HELLO`
again. That is #45413.

Reproduced first on a debug build of `main` and on the 1.4.3 release
binary with the same scripts (the table above), then on this branch.

New tests, in the existing files for each area:

`test/js/web/fetch/fetch-keepalive.test.ts`
- a GET that a reused https connection answers with: bytes that are not
a TLS record, a record that does not authenticate, half a response head
and then such bytes, close_notify, FIN, RST. Each is sent again, and the
origin sees exactly `connection 1 request 1`, `connection 1 request 2`,
`connection 2 request 1`.
- a POST, and a GET whose response body has started, are **not** sent
again and reject with `EPROTO`.
- a GET through a reused tunnel when the session with the https proxy
breaks is sent again over a second tunnel.

`test/js/valkey/reliability/connection-failures.test.ts`
- the client reconnects when the session breaks in place of a reply
(with the next command issued straight from the rejection) and while
idle. `onclose` never fires, and the command in flight still rejects
with `EPROTO: ...WRONG_VERSION_NUMBER`.
- with `autoReconnect: false`, a `GET` in flight and an `INFO` queued
behind it both reject with the TLS reason, and `onclose` fires once.
- a TLS 1.3 server that requires a client certificate fails a client
that has none at its first dial, with
`TLSV1_ALERT_CERTIFICATE_REQUIRED`.

| | without the `src/` change | with it |
|---|---|---|
| `fetch-keepalive.test.ts` | 4 fail (the three TLS breaks, the https
proxy) | 58 pass |
| `connection-failures.test.ts`, the 4 new tests | 2 fail (the two
reconnects) | 4 pass |

The existing tests that pin the errors of a broken session pass with the
change: `test/js/bun/net/tls-fatal-error-closes.test.ts` (every owner
still closes, same errors) 38 pass,
`test/js/node/tls/renegotiation.test.ts` (a refused renegotiation,
`fetch` and `RedisClient`) 57 pass.

The close_notify / FIN / RST rows, the two "not sent again" rows and the
last two Redis tests pass on both builds on purpose: they pin what must
not change.

The bug is newer than the latest release, so `USE_SYSTEM_BUN=1` passes
these tests. "Fails without the fix" was shown by building `main`'s
`src/` with the new tests.

Each condition on the Redis side was removed in turn to see a test fail:
without `status == Connected` the server that refuses the certificate is
dialed again, without the `autoReconnect` condition the queued `INFO`
gets `Connection closed`, and with an event loop scope around the
rejections the command issued from the rejection goes out on the dead
socket. The new Redis tests also pass under
`BUN_JSC_validateExceptionChecks=1`.

The files next to these that go through the same two `on_handshake`
handlers, for real handshake failures (wrong name, untrusted chain,
`checkServerIdentity`), also pass:
`test/js/valkey/valkey-tls-verify.test.ts` 14 pass,
`test/js/web/fetch/fetch.tls.test.ts` 64 pass. My machine has no IPv6,
so for that run their servers listened on `127.0.0.1`. The one failure
there, `trusts a self-issued certificate without keyCertSign only when
ca holds that very certificate` (`CERT_SIGNATURE_FAILURE` where
`UNABLE_TO_VERIFY_LEAF_SIGNATURE` is expected), is the same on `main`
without this change.

`connection-failures.test.ts` has one failure on my machine that is not
from this PR: `close() over rediss:// while the peer has stopped reading
closes the socket at once` fails the same way on `main` without this
change. The tests there that need a Redis server were skipped (no
Docker).

