From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenSec <contactus@opensec.in>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 02:05:33 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] GC timer: no idle full collection that would find the heap as
 the previous one left it

The idle full collections at 10 s, 2 min and 10 min of quiet ran whether or not anything had happened since the
previous one. Pi at its prompt allocates nothing after the 10 s one, so the 2-min one marked the same live objects,
freed nothing (footprint 20.3-20.5 MB before, 20.4-20.7 MB after) and cost 33 M instructions, about 14 ms of CPU on an
M5, plus the scavenger's purges after it: the largest wake-up left in an idle Pi.

An idle full collection is now skipped when it cannot do anything the previous one did not: nothing has been allocated
since the previous idle full collection was requested (JSC's running total, which no collection changes), that
collection has finished, and the heap holds no code that can age (no LLInt, baseline, DFG or FTL CodeBlock: one whose
lease ran out since the previous collection would be let go in this one; code compiled ahead of time never ages). Both
start from this timer, so they scan the same stack. The forced allocator collect still runs, for memory other threads
freed. The last one (10 min) always runs: before it JSC also lets go of code and bytecode it can get back.

What a skipped collection does not do: count towards the age of unlinked bytecode held only weakly (dropped after seven
full collections without running), so such bytecode goes one full collection later; and collect an object kept alive by
native pending activity that ended without the program allocating (a socket or subprocess that finished with no JS
callback), which goes at the next collection instead.
