From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: OpenSec <contactus@opensec.in>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 03:17:27 +0530
Subject: [PATCH] mimalloc: the scavenger sleeps while there is nothing to give
 back

With nothing scheduled the scavenger still woke for good after 30 s (doubling up to an hour while nothing
interrupted it): a pass over the arenas, and a second sweep of every thread that stayed parked since its last
sweep. Pi at its prompt: the first such wake-up after an answer gives back what the GC threads freed into the main
thread's pages and left when they ended (0.6 MB, M5), each one after that finds nothing (wake-ups at 30, 90, 210 s
... after the last activity, for the life of the process).

What a second sweep of a parked thread can give back comes from another thread: a block freed into one of its pages,
or a page abandoned when a thread ends (both are swept with the parked threads). Those now set a flag
(`xfree_seen`, a load on the free path once it is set); the scavenger notes when it saw it, and sweeps a parked
thread again 30 s after its last sweep only if the flag was seen since that sweep. With no thread due for that, and
nothing scheduled, it waits with no timeout. Everything else that leaves memory to give back already wakes it: a
purge scheduled by a free of arena memory, a thread that parks, and stop(). The first free into another thread's
page while it waits with no timeout wakes it (seq_cst on both sides, so either the scavenger sees the flag before it
waits or the free sees it waiting).

Measured with mimalloc's tests on macOS (all 40 pass, test-park-again among them: what another thread frees is given
back while the owner stays parked, here after 30.5 s) and a test of Pi's pattern (main thread parks, a helper thread
ends at 2 s, another thread frees main's blocks at 4 s, 130 s): the same bytes purged at the end (0.23 MB), at 30 s
instead of 32 s, and no wake-up of the scavenger after it, against wake-ups at 32, 92 and 212 s before.

What no longer happens while nothing is going on: a parked thread's retired pages and the large pages its sweep kept
under purge_holes_large_floor counted down one step per idle sweep (16 and 256 steps): with the doubling wait that
took days of idling. They go at the thread's next park, as for a thread that is not idle.

Replaces patches/mimalloc/scavenger-idle-backoff.patch.
